# Interactive Controls (Checkboxes & Radios)

HD2 supports **clickable checkboxes and radio buttons**. You (or an agent) write them in Markdown; a reader toggles them in the HD editor; the selected state is saved back into the document so an agent can read the choices later. This is built for review-and-approve workflows — an agent proposes changes, a human approves a subset, the agent applies exactly what was approved.

**HD2 only.** Interactive controls are a capability of the experimental `.hd2` flavor. They are not available in `.hd` documents.

## Why not real form controls?

HD forbids form elements (`input`, `button`, `select`, …) by design. So HD2 does *not* store a checkbox control — it stores the **state** in data attributes and renders an interactive control only inside the editor. The saved markup is inert, allow-listed HTML, which is also easier for an agent to read than a disabled form input.

## Checkboxes

Checkboxes use the standard **GFM task list** syntax. Put a bracket marker at the start of a list item:

```markdown
- [x] Apply the database migration
- [ ] Update the config file
- [ ] Restart the worker
```

`[x]` is checked; `[ ]` is unchecked. In the editor each item shows a real checkbox you can click. Checkboxes are independent — any number can be checked at once.

## Radio buttons

Radios use an HD2 convention that parallels checkboxes — **parentheses instead of brackets** at the start of a list item:

```markdown
- ( ) Roll back fully
- (x) Roll back partially
- ( ) Leave in place
```

`(x)` is the selected option; `( )` is unselected. Radios behave as a single-select group with two important rules:

-   **Consecutive radios are one group.** They are mutually exclusive — selecting one clears the others. A blank line (or any content) between two radios starts a separate group.
    
-   **Selecting is reversible.** Clicking the already-selected radio *deselects* it, so a group can legitimately have nothing selected — useful when "no decision yet" is a meaningful state during review.
    

### Naming a group (optional)

A group can carry an optional name so an agent can reference it unambiguously (e.g. "the answer to the rollback question"). A named group is stored as a small HTML block rather than paren-markers, because the name has nowhere to live in the `( )` syntax:

```html
<ul data-type="radiogroup" data-group="rollback-strategy">
  <li data-type="radio" data-checked="true">Roll back fully</li>
  <li data-type="radio" data-checked="false">Roll back partially</li>
</ul>
```

Unnamed groups stay as clean `- ( )` Markdown; add a name only when you need one.

## How state is stored

Whatever is selected when the document is saved is written into the file. In the raw `.hd2` source that is simply the marker itself — `[x]`, `[ ]`, `(x)`, `( )`. Internally, and in a named radio group, the state is the `data-checked` attribute:

| Control | Unset in source | Set in source | Editor |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Checkbox | `- [ ]` | `- [x]` | Clickable checkbox; any number checked. |
| Radio | `- ( )` | `- (x)` | Clickable circle; one-per-group, deselectable. |

Because the state is part of the saved content, the review loop is closed: the agent reads the current markers, sees exactly what the human approved, and acts only on those.

## The remediation workflow

1.  **Agent proposes.** It generates a `.hd2` file listing recommended changes as checkboxes (approve any) and decisions as radio groups (pick one).
    
2.  **Human reviews.** Opening the file in the HD editor shows real, clickable controls. The reviewer checks the changes to apply and picks options where a single choice is needed.
    
3.  **State saves.** Each toggle is an edit, so the selections are written straight back into the `.hd2` file.
    
4.  **Agent applies.** It re-reads the file and applies exactly the checked items and selected options — nothing more.
    

## Grouping is by adjacency

A radio group is a **run of consecutive radio lines**. There is no explicit group syntax — adjacency defines the group:

-   Radio lines with nothing between them are **one** mutually-exclusive group.
    
-   A **blank line** — or any other content — between two radios starts a new group. This is how you split a set of options into separate questions.
    
-   At most one item in a group can be selected (zero is allowed). Toggling only ever affects the clicked item's own group — never a neighbouring one.
    

Use a [named group](#) (the `data-group` form above) only when you need to label a group for an agent to reference; otherwise adjacency is all you need.

## Authoring notes

-   Each control is a **whole line** — a list item whose marker is at the start: `- [ ]` or `- ( )`.
    
-   **In the editor**, typing the marker at the start of a line creates the control: `[]`  makes a checkbox, `()`  makes a radio. A control is only ever created by typing its marker — **Enter never auto-creates one**.
    
-   **Enter** behaves by position: on the last item of a group it ends the group and drops you to a normal new line; on an item that has items after it, it **splits the group** there (the following items become a separate group); on an empty item it removes it.
    
-   Typing a second control right after the first **joins the same group**. To start a separate group, leave a blank line before it.
    
-   Don't mix checkbox and radio markers in the same run; each run is one kind of control.
    
-   Checkbox syntax is standard GFM, so agents already know how to produce it. Radio syntax (`( )`) is HD2-specific — tell the agent the convention when you want single-select groups.
    
-   Controls survive a full round-trip: opening, toggling, and saving re-emits the same clean Markdown, changing only the marker state.
